Original commit body from bc00dd4c4c4133aab701c6f005425930a4e12ed7:

Measurement 0002 reported Go at 162/210 with "100% of Go's method-kind surface
unattributed" and concluded Go was "the natural next target for the same fix"
as Python's receiver-typing work. BOTH HALVES ARE WITHDRAWN, and the conclusion
was the expensive half: Go receiver typing already exists and works, so a
session acting on that sentence would have built a mechanism the analyzer has.

THE 45/45 MISS WAS THE PROBE'S RECEIVER SPELLING. The fixture emitted every Go
method call as a parenthesized composite literal. go.py types a receiver only
when the operand is a bare identifier, so that one spelling carries no receiver
evidence. Verified in one file, one analyze_go run — only the spelling varies:

  (&http.Client{}).Get(u)             -> go:external:0-0:Get:unresolved
  var c http.Client; c.Get(u)         -> go:net/http:0-0:Get:unresolved
  c := &http.Client{}; c.Get(u)       -> go:net/http:0-0:Get:unresolved
  func(c *http.Client){ c.Get(u) }    -> go:net/http:0-0:Get:unresolved

The instrument now probes both spellings and KEEPS the parenthesized one, so
the analyzer hole stays measured rather than hidden by the fix to the probe.

AND THE CORRECTED 198/210 IS ALSO INFLATED, SO THIS ARM NOW REPORTS TWO
NUMBERS. The probe's import path is the catalogue's own module slot, which for
a third-party entry is a package IDENTIFIER, not a module PATH — so the fixture
writes `import ("gin")`, a line no Go program contains, and the entry scores
reachable against a hint production can never emit:

  lookup_with_module("JSON", "github.com/gin-gonic/gin", cc="method") -> None
  lookup_with_module("JSON", "gin",                      cc="method")
      -> gin.Context.JSON

53 of 210 entries are reachable ONLY through such a fabricated import, 20 of
them method-kind. Production-faithful: 178/210 (84.8%).

The instrument DISCLOSES its synthesised import paths rather than classifying
them. Separating a real single-segment stdlib path (os, net, testing) from a
fabricated one (gin, echo) needs Go's stdlib package list, which go.yaml does
not carry — it marks the framework rows with a YAML comment and nothing
machine-readable — and shipping a guessed classifier would put a wrong verdict
in the headline.

TWO DIFFERENT 53s, DISCLOSED SO THE COINCIDENCE IS NOT MISREAD. The plan
separately records go.yaml carrying "53 of 210 entries violating the
strict-stdlib rule". The sets overlap in 50 and differ by `filepath` (stdlib,
written unimportably) versus `golang.org/x/sys/execabs` (third-party, but
path-shaped so faithfully imported). Two routes agreeing on a count is not two
routes agreeing on a set.

ALSO RECORDED, both verified live and neither fixed here:
  - Go's semantic import versioning erases the import path outright. The
    package alias is the last path segment, so github.com/labstack/echo/v4
    registers `v4` and `echo` is never bound; echo.Context.JSON and
    fiber.App.Listen both fall to go:external. Blast radius is the Go call
    graph, not just the io-boundary map. Unmeasured on any corpus, and
    structurally invisible to this instrument, whose fixture writes
    `import ("fiber")` — a path with no /vN element.
  - The 9 residuals are MIS-CREDITS, not misses, and they invert sign between
    instrument and product: net/http.Client.{Get,Post,Head},
    log/slog.Logger.{Debug,Info,Warn,Error,Log} and testing.B.TempDir each
    produce a chain under a same-package FUNCTION row. Confirmed through the
    CLI: a *testing.B receiver is credited testing.T.TempDir, and
    `tr := &http.Transport{}; tr.Do(..)` is credited net/http.Client.Do — a
    false positive, since Transport has no Do.
  - call_construct CANNOT fix that. Go stamps it "method" on every
    selector-expression call, package-level free functions included, so
    http.Get(u) and c.Get(u) produce byte-identical
    (name, module_hint, call_construct) triples. Any predicate keyed on that
    field is keyed on a constant.

Docs-and-instrument only; no production code path changes.

